<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Morgannwg</h1><p class="st-meta">942 CE – 1091 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A south-east Welsh kingdom formed by the union of Glywysing and Gwent, whose name survives in Glamorgan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Morgannwg, 'Morgan's land', is traditionally named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morgan-ab-owain">Morgan Hen</a> ab Owain, who united the older kingdoms of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-glywysing">Glywysing</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-gwent">Gwent</a> around 942. Earlier rulers of the region are known from the charters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-llandaff">Book of Llandaff</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom covered the fertile vale and uplands between the Tywi and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-wye">Wye</a>, with churches such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llandaff">Llandaff</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llancarfan">Llancarfan</a>. Its kings dealt with English rulers, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morgan-ab-owain">Morgan Hen</a> attended Aethelstan's court. After his death in 974 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-gwent">Gwent</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-glywysing">Glywysing</a> separated again at times, and the kingdom was later ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gruffydd-ap-llywelyn">Gruffydd ap Llywelyn</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caradog-ap-gruffydd">Caradog ap Gruffydd</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iestyn-ap-gwrgant">Iestyn ap Gwrgant</a>, the last native ruler, was overthrown around 1090 by the Norman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-fitzhamon">Robert Fitzhamon</a>, who created the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-glamorgan">Lordship of Glamorgan</a>. The name survives in the counties of Glamorgan.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 942 CE – 1091 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">British Isles</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:78.45%;width:2.97%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>942 CE – 1091 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Morgan Hen, Caradog ap Gruffydd, Iestyn ap Gwrgant</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Welsh</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Morgannwg, Kingdom of Glamorgan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1016&amp;focus=kingdom-of-morgannwg" aria-label="Kingdom of Morgannwg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4607 392 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Morgannwg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4927 648L4932 643M4927 648L4923 651M4927 648L4928 651"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4932 643h0M4923 651h0M4928 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4927 648h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4927 648h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1016&amp;focus=kingdom-of-morgannwg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
